# Reference manuals, fetched locally to check the generated COBOL and JCL
# against the conventions rather than against assumptions. Read, never copied
# from, and never committed: they are IBM's to distribute, not this repo's.
vendor-docs/

# Bundled language server for the VS Code extension, built by its own script.
packages/vscode-extension/server/

# Agent instruction files are a local convenience, not part of the project.
# CONTRIBUTING.md carries the rules that apply to everybody.
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md
.claude/

# External validation corpora, downloaded by `tools/horizontal-fetch.ts`.
#
# Somebody else's COBOL, fetched at a pinned revision so this compiler can be
# measured against programs nobody wrote for it. It stays out of git for two
# separate reasons and either would be enough.
#
# Licensing. X-COBOL is 5,195 files gathered from 168 repositories, published
# under a CC-BY licence that covers the *compilation* rather than the contents;
# none of those 168 authors agreed to be vendored into an MIT repository. The
# gate in `packages/horizontal-validation/src/licence.ts` is what decides per
# corpus whether material may be committed, and `derived-only` means statistics
# and hashes leave the cache and nothing else does.
#
# Size. The one archive is 50MB before it is unpacked.
#
# `validation/corpus-lock.json` is committed and pins exactly which bytes any
# published number was measured against, so an ignored cache costs no
# reproducibility.
validation/cache/

# Working papers: this project's audits of itself and its ticket lists.
#
# They are the record of how the work was decided and they stay out of the
# public repository, and one of them documents rewrites of this repository's own
# history, which is not a thing to publish. Kept on disk, referenced by date in
# commit messages where the reasoning matters, and never tracked.
#
# `tools/build-docs.ts` already refuses to publish a working paper left in
# `docs/`; this is the second half of that, and the one that decides what a
# clone contains.
docs/working/

# The benchmarks' own COBOL, held apart from the specifications.
#
# `tools/horizontal-materialise.ts` splits every semantic benchmark record into
# a spec (prose, inputs, expected outputs) and the reference solution. The
# spec is committed and is what a BankTS implementation is written from; this is
# the other half, and it is derived from the ignored cache rather than authored,
# so nothing is lost by keeping it out. Its absence from git is what makes the
# contamination boundary checkable by a reader rather than promised by a
# comment. See docs/validation/horizontal-validation.md.
validation/sealed/
